<v Elizabeth Joseph>They were understandably upset. My dad was very educated, but a Montana cowboy and he told my brothers to get their rifles and they were coming after me.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>他们理所当然地很不安。我爸爸受过良好的教育，但是个蒙大拿牛仔，他告诉我的兄弟们拿上猎枪，他们要来找我。

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:06.780
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:17.720
<v Elizabeth Joseph>My mother's a very educated, smart women and she was heartbroken, but she wasn't willing to give the association with her only daughter to maintain a point.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>我母亲是一个受过良好教育、聪明的女人，她心碎了，但她不愿为了一个观点而放弃与她唯一女儿的联系。

00:49:17.720 --> 00:49:27.790
<v Ira  Glass>Can I ask you to just explain kind of the practical terms of it? Like does everybody live in separate houses? Does everybody live in the same house? How often do you see Alex?
<v Ira  Glass>我能请你解释一下它实际的情况吗？比如每个人住分开的房子吗？每个人住同一个房子吗？你多久见一次亚历克斯？

00:49:27.790 --> 00:49:47.050
<v Elizabeth Joseph>I see Alex every day for sure. Structurally, I have a home that I share with another wife just because it's so huge. But Margaret has her own place, Beau has our own place, Joanna has her own place. But we're all right close together.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>我每天肯定能见到亚历克斯。在结构上，我有一个与另一个妻子共享的家，仅仅是因为它太大了。但玛格丽特（Margaret）有自己的地方，博（Beau）有自己的地方，乔安娜（Joanna）有自己的地方。但我们都离得很近。

00:49:47.050 --> 00:50:21.200
<v Elizabeth Joseph>It's not like if he's at Diane and [? Don's ?] house, I walk right in the front door and make myself at home. I mean, we enjoy each other's company. Margaret likes to say that he's more fun when there's more than one of us just because that's his nature. And yeah, there's three of us that have anniversaries real close together and it's a longstanding tradition for the four of us to go out to dinner together near those anniversary. You know, the family spends a lot of time together. The kids are very fond of one another from the different mothers.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>如果他在戴安（Diane）和[？唐（Don）？]家，并不是我能直接走前门进去把自己当做家。我的意思是，我们享受彼此的陪伴。玛格丽特喜欢说，当我们在场的人不止一个时，他更有趣，因为这就是他的天性。是的，我们有三个人的结婚纪念日非常接近，我们四个人在那些纪念日附近一起出去吃晚餐是一个由来已久的传统。你知道，家庭在一起花很多时间。孩子们非常喜欢不同母亲生出来的孩子。

00:50:21.200 --> 00:50:23.840
<v Ira Glass>So how many kids are there in the family?
<v Ira Glass>所以家里有多少个孩子？

00:50:23.840 --> 00:50:28.190
<v Elizabeth Joseph>20. And I'd say about half are grown.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>20个。我想大概有一半已经长大了。

00:50:28.190 --> 00:50:31.910
<v Ira Glass>And then, how often will he actually stay with you if I can ask you questions that personal.
<v Ira Glass>然后，如果我能问你这么隐私的问题，他实际上多久和你待一次？

00:50:31.910 --> 00:50:59.600
<v Elizabeth Joseph>Yeah, we actually stay with him. He's got his own quarters. But over the years it's varied. When I was 20 I had one kind of sexual appetite and now that I'm in my-- I hate to say this-- mid 40s, it's a little different. But in talking to my monogamous friends, I have that kind of exchange with him as often or more often than they do. You know, roughly average once a week. It just depends.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>是的，我们实际上和他待在一起。他有自己的居所。但这些年来情况各不相同。我20岁时有一种性欲，现在我——我讨厌这么说——40多岁了，情况有点不同。但在和我的单配偶朋友交谈时，我和他进行那种交流的频率和她们一样多，甚至更多。你知道，大约平均每周一次。这取决于情况。

00:50:59.600 --> 00:51:30.070
<v Ira Glass>I've heard Mormon men talk about polygamy and talking about the advantage of it as being this. They say that there's certain things that a woman looks to a man for in terms of wanting to talk about things and wanting to be close regarding certain kinds of issues and have a certain kind of close friendship that a lot of men just they don't talk that way. They don't relate to other people that way. And so one of the advantages of polygamy is that the women, the wives, can get that from the other wives.
<v Ira Glass>我听说过摩门教男人谈论一夫多妻制，认为它的优势在于这。他们说，在某些关于想和人谈论事情、想在某些问题上亲近以及拥有某种亲密友谊方面，女性会向男人求助，而很多男人只是——他们不那样说话。他们不那样与人交往。所以一夫多妻制的优势之一是女性，妻子们，可以从其他妻子那里得到这种东西。

00:51:30.070 --> 00:52:06.400
<v Elizabeth Joseph>That's one of the huge advantages. That's a really nice thing. I mean, some of our funnest times have been when he's been like on a business trip and we'll get together and have a tequila party or something and just laugh our heads off. Just have a good time. Because the way the wives feel about one another is we're just extraordinarily proud of one another. We're extremely proud to be associated with one another. And we've had women come into the situation and look into it. And we didn't like them, and we got rid of them. We're women. We're good at it.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>那是巨大的优势之一。那真是件好事。我的意思是，我们最有趣的时候是他出差时，我们会聚在一起喝龙舌兰酒什么的，笑得前仰后合。玩得很开心。因为妻子们对彼此的感觉是我们对彼此感到非常自豪。我们为能与彼此联系在一起感到非常自豪。而且有女性进入这种情况并进行审视。我们不喜欢她们，我们就把她们踢出去了。我们是女人。我们擅长这个。

00:52:06.400 --> 00:52:12.110
<v Ira Glass>Do you have some sort of veto power over whether a woman actually gets to marry in?
<v Ira Glass>对于一个女人是否真正能嫁进来，你们有某种否决权吗？

00:52:12.110 --> 00:52:30.280
<v Elizabeth Joseph>It's de facto, OK? The rule is he'll marry whom he pleases. But he has learned over the years that if we don't like them, his relationship with them is going nowhere. So we've chased off our share.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>那是事实上的，OK？规则是他会娶他高兴的人。但他多年来已经学会了，如果我们不喜欢她们，他与她们的关系是不会有结果的。所以我们已经赶走了一部分。

00:52:30.280 --> 00:52:35.470
<v Ira Glass>And do people get jealous? Do you get jealous?
<v Ira Glass>人们会嫉妒吗？你会嫉妒吗？

00:52:35.470 --> 00:53:19.320
<v Elizabeth Joseph>Well, you know, us American girls are raised to be very insecure and jealous. Neither of which is a trait that you would want to embrace. So in the early years I think as with any marriage, it takes a while to feel your way and get that security established. And yeah, there were times when I'd go well, why-- gee, you know, I can see way he likes Margaret. She does that so well and I don't. But then after a while you go, yeah, but look what I do that Margaret doesn't. And he does that. That's his job is to give you that security that he loves you for what you are. And 25 years into this deal and we've sort of got it down pat. We're not too threatened at all.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>嗯，你知道，我们美国女孩从小就很不安全且容易嫉妒。这两者都不是你想要拥有的特质。所以早年间我想就像任何婚姻一样，需要一点时间去摸索和建立安全感。是的，曾经有几次我会想，好吧，哎呀，你知道，我能看出为什么他喜欢玛格丽特。她那方面做得很好，而我做不好。但后来你就会想，是的，但看看我做的事玛格丽特没做。他就是那样。这是他的工作，给你那种安全感，即他爱你本来的样子。在这段关系25年后，我们已经把它摸透了。我们根本不感到太大的威胁。

00:53:19.320 --> 00:53:31.800
<v Ira Glass>Is this something that you think should be widely recommended to people? Or do you feel like that you are just in a special situation with a special man and it's not applicable to other people's lives?
<v Ira Glass>你认为这应该广泛推荐给人们吗？或者你觉得你只是处在一个有特殊男人的特殊情况中，它不适用于其他人的生活？

00:53:31.800 --> 00:54:02.720
<v Elizabeth Joseph>It's only a certain kind of people. For example, I had a son who he had a family. And I have a son. But anyway, a girl came to him who'd grown up in our community and she said what I said. You're obviously a good father and husband. Can I marry you? And he really liked this girl. But he was blown away. He goes, I don't know if I can do this. And of course I knew him very well, my son. I said, Stewart, you're one of the few guys I think can. And he lasted like two months.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>这只适用于特定类型的人。例如，我有一个儿子，他有一个家庭。我有一个儿子。但不管怎样，一个在我们社区长大的女孩来到他面前，她说了我曾经说的话。你显然是个好父亲和丈夫。我可以嫁给你吗？他真的很喜欢这个女孩。但他被震撼了。他说，我不知道我能不能做到。我当然非常了解他，我的儿子。我说，斯图尔特（Stewart），你是我认为少数几个能做到的男人之一。他只坚持了大约两个月。

00:54:02.720 --> 00:54:04.340
<v Ira Glass>And why do you think it didn't work for him?
<v Ira Glass>你为什么认为这对他不起作用？

00:54:04.340 --> 00:54:15.100
<v Elizabeth Joseph>Well, usually two wives is very difficult. You really need a third to balance it out. But usually, the most common scenario is the two women will gang up on the guy.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>嗯，通常两个妻子是非常困难的。你真的需要第三个来平衡它。但通常，最常见的情况是两个女人会联手对付这个男人。

00:54:15.100 --> 00:54:20.360
<v Ira Glass>But you're thinking like 1 out of 10 men that you know could do it or like it's more m 1 out a 100?
<v Ira Glass>但你认为是你认识的男人中大概十分之一能做到，还是更像是百分之一？

00:54:20.360 --> 00:54:22.710
<v Elizabeth Joseph>More like 1 out of 1,000, if not 10,000.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>更像是千分之一，甚至万分之一。

00:54:22.710 --> 00:54:24.290
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:54:24.290 --> 00:54:35.080
<v Elizabeth   Joseph>It's not easy. That's why most of his boys will tell you they wouldn't entertain it because they know how difficult it is. Because they watch their dad do it.
<v Elizabeth   Joseph>这不容易。这就是为什么他大多数的儿子会告诉你他们不会考虑这样做，因为他们知道这有多难。因为他们看着他们的父亲这样做。

00:54:35.080 --> 00:54:40.530
<v Ira Glass>And the main difficulty is just keeping everybody satisfied and happy?
<v Ira Glass>主要的困难只是让每个人都感到满意和快乐吗？

00:54:40.530 --> 00:54:54.360
<v Elizabeth Joseph>Well, yeah. You got to be way smart. Like he says, how would you like eight women working your inventory 24 hours a day we? And we do. We do. We're strong willed, independent, say what we think.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>嗯，是的。你得非常聪明。就像他说的，如果你有八个女人24小时盯着你的库存，你会感觉如何？我们确实这样。我们意志坚强、独立，想到什么就说什么。

00:54:54.360 --> 00:55:26.720
<v Elizabeth Joseph>I haven't met anybody that could do what Alex has done. I've met polygamists, long-term polygamists, who I admire and respect for what they've done. But in terms of demonstration, it doesn't come anything close to his family.
<v Elizabeth Joseph>我还没遇到过能做亚历克斯所做之事的人。我遇到过我钦佩和尊重他们所作所为的一夫多妻者，长期的那种。但从示范的角度来看，没有什么能比得上他的家庭。

00:55:26.720 --> 00:56:06.970
<v Ira              Glass>Elizabeth Joseph in Utah.
<v Ira              Glass>犹他州的伊丽莎白·约瑟夫。

